 "bloques": [
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "The consonant from below",
   "texto": [
    "A syllable can end in consonant. That final consonant is written **debajo** from the block and is called **받침** (batchim).",
    "한 es ㅎ + ㅏ + ㄴ = «han». 국 es ㄱ + ㅜ + ㄱ = «guk». Together: **한국**, Korea.",
    "At the end of the syllable, it is no longer mute: it sounds **ng**, as in «tango». And it sounds like **l**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Seven categories of final sound",
   "texto": [
    "In the final position, several consonants are neutralized in **siete categorías de sonido** and articulated without an audible explosion. This does not mean that there are only seven final letters: the spelling retains the consonant and the groups, and the sound can change when it is linked to the next syllable.",
    "They end up with a category similar to t, although they are written differently."
   ]
  },
