  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Link and changes",
   "texto": [
    "When a syllable ends in consonant and the next one starts with support, the consonant usually links with the vocal: \"Psst.\" - \"Psst.\" The result cannot always be deduced by looking at only one isolated consonant.",
    "In the United States, it is nasalized and heard as m. There are more sound changes; you will learn them by patterns and with frequent words."
